{
  "dom_id": "step-79781",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Representing \"Or\" Inequalities on Number Lines",
  "problem": "Represent the inequality $x < -2$ or $x \\geq  1$ on a number line.",
  "worked_solution": "The inequality $x < -2$ or $x \\geq  1$ means that $x$ can take any value less than $-2,$ or greater than or equal to $1.$\n\nTo represent this on a number line, we graph the two separate inequalities:\n\n- the inequality $x < -2$ consists of an open circle at $-2$ and an arrow to the left.\n\n- the inequality $x \\geq 1$ consists of a closed circle at $1$ and an arrow to the right.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151668/assets/a1067762a65fca32ca847a58dd47b07258f4ce58df20e45435df8a0a9e5d6f90.png)",
